<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Ochterlony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir David Ochterlony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Ochterlony (1758–1825) was a Boston-born general of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814–1816 he was the most successful British commander, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-singh-thapa">Amar Singh Thapa</a> to surrender in 1815 and advancing towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> in 1816, which led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> to ratify the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a>. He later served as Resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and a monument to him stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-ochterlony.jpg" alt="David Ochterlony" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur William Devis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Ochterlony.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
